  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 114 PM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nSurface high pressure continues to expand over the Great Lakes \nearly this afternoon, providing the area with cooler east- \nnortheasterly surface winds. While dewpoints started the day \naround 70, a persistent influx of a drier low-level airmass has \ncontinued to drop values through the 60s this morning. The \nremainder of the day will be rather pleasant, with mostly sunny \nconditions and temperatures peaking from around 80 inland to the\nmid and upper 70s closer to the lake. Similar conditions are \nexpected for Labor Day, as the Great Lakes surface high remains \ndominant.\n\nEast-northeasterly onshore winds across southern Lake Michigan \nare not terribly strong (10-15 knots). However, the fetch has \nallowed waves to become choppy across southern Lake Michigan, \nwith wave heights generally in the 2 to 4 foot range along the \nnortheastern Illinois shores. These conditions are expected to \npersist tonight, making for a Moderate Swim Risk at northeastern\nIllinois Lake Michigan beaches.\n\nLate Monday into Tuesday, the mid/upper Pacific Northwest \ntrough/low is expected to eject east-northeastward across the \nnorthern Rockies. As it does, models and ensembles remain in \ngeneral agreement regarding the development and northeastward \nshift of a seasonably strong surface low across Manitoba. While \nthis system will remain far removed from our area, it will act \nto drive the mid-level ridge axis residing over the Plains back \neastward into the western Great Lakes for Tuesday. An \naccompanying shift back to southerly low-level flow will thus \nresult in warming weather across our region into midweek. \nTemperatures for Tuesday into Wednesday are expected to climb \nback well into the 80s (potentially near 90 in some locations). \nHumidity will also be on the increase, though it is not expected\nto reach the magnitude experienced a couple of days ago.\n\nThis period of warmer weather is expected to remain short-\nlived, as the cold front associated with the potent Canadian low\nbegins to sag southward across our area Wednesday into early \nThursday. A period of showers and thunderstorms will be possible\n(50%+) with this front from late Tuesday night through \nWednesday night. However, the better synoptic-scale forcing does\nnot look to coincide with the surface front until potentially \nlater Wednesday night into Thursday morning. By this time, the \nfront should be shifting south of much of our area into central \nIllinois and Indiana. For this reason, shower and thunderstorm \nactivity could remain more scattered in coverage in our area on \nWednesday.\n\nSurface high pressure will build back into the region for \nThursday and Friday in the wake of Wednesday's cold frontal \npassage. This will result in a return to cooler, more pleasant \nweather through the end of the workweek. As we head into next \nweekend, there is a signal for a larger-scale mid-level trough \nand surface cold front to shift into the central CONUS later in \nthe weekend. However, specifics regarding the pattern remain \nlargely unclear at this time owing to increasing ensemble \nspread. Nevertheless, chances for showers and thunderstorms \nreturn to the forecast for next weekend.\n\nKJB",
